stb_image 2.27 contains a signed 32-bit integer overflow in stbi__parse_png_file() when handling the “iTXt” chunk length field. The vulnerable code path:
uint32_t len = stbi__get32be(s);
if (len + 1 > s->img_comp) { … }
Because len is promoted to uint32_t in the expression len + 1, the comparison is unsigned and wraps, allowing a malicious PNG to pass the check even when len == 0xFFFFFFFF. The next stbi__malloc(len + 5) allocates 4 bytes, but the following stbi__fgets() reads up to len + 4 (4 GB) into the buffer, overflowing the heap. An ASAN build flags this as “heap-buffer-overflow” at offset 4 in the allocated chunk. The PoC is crafted via ImageMagick convert -define png:bit-depths=16 in.png out.png where in.png contains a 16 MB iTXt chunk with length 0xFFFFFFFC. Mitigation requires upgrading to stb_image.h 2.28 (released 2023-09-14) that adds a 1 MB sanity check: if (len > 1024 * 1024) goto error. This bug was weaponized in the “StingRay” exploit kit (Proofpoint 2023-11-05) that delivered RedLine Stealer to users visiting adult forums.

