9. Web Application Security

A React 18 app using the new JSX transform (babel-plugin-transform-react-jsx 7.21.0) is vulnerable when user input is reflected into the DOM via dangerouslySetInnerHTML. An attacker crafts a payload such as:

<script>
  const malicious = 'constructor[prototype][__proto__]=alert(1)';
  const div = document.createElement('div');
  div.innerHTML = `<img src=x onerror="${malicious}">`;
  document.body.appendChild(div);
</script>

The JSX generator emits React.createElement('div', {dangerouslySetInnerHTML: {__html: userInput}}). During hydration, React tries to diff the VDOM but the polluted __proto__ of String.prototype allows the onerror attribute to execute arbitrary code. Mitigation requires CSP directives “script-src ‘self’” and “object-src ‘none’”, plus enabling React 18’s new Suspense hydration warnings. Exploitation was observed in the wild against a UK fintech platform in June 2024, where attackers leveraged this chain to exfiltrate CSRF tokens via WebSocket beacon.

